    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Njimbe's constitution rotated the throne among the rulers of four provinces, Kaye, Boke, Selage and Kabango, the ruler of Kaye succeeding when the king died.</li><li>Loango exported considerable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> to the European market and thousands of metres of cloth to Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> in the early seventeenth century; from the late seventeenth century its Vili merchants also traded in slaves on a considerable scale.</li><li>After King Buatu died in 1787 no king was elected for over a hundred years; a priest called the Nganga Mvumbi, who guarded the unburied body of the dead king, kept what remained of royal authority.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>European ships never built permanent factories at Loango: they anchored offshore and dealt with royal officials called mafouks, who managed trade in the king's interest and kept direct European influence at arm's length.</p></aside>
    </section>

