    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo.jpg" alt="Afonso I of Kongo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo">Afonso I of Kongo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manikongo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Affricoll9, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Loango was created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> people on the Atlantic coast north of the more powerful Kingdom of Kongo, in what is now the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, southern Gabon and Cabinda. Its origins are obscure: the oldest complex society in the region was at Madingo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayes">Kayes</a>, settled by the first century CE, but the archaeology says little more before the sixteenth century. Loango is absent from the titles of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo">Afonso I of Kongo</a> in 1535. The earliest documentary reference is a mention around 1561 by Sebastião de Souto, a priest in Kongo, that King Diogo I (1545–61) had sent missionaries to convert Loango; in 1585 Duarte Lopes, Kongo's ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, called it a friend of Kongo and a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> visitors of the 1630s or 1640s recorded the first traditional account of its origin, reported by Olfert Dapper. The region had held small polities, Mayumba, Kilongo, Piri and Wansi among them, at war with each other. The founder, from Nzari in Kakongo, a vassal of Kongo, defeated his rivals through skilful alliances and built his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-loango">Buali</a> in the province of Piri, from which the name Muvili derived. Andrew Battel, there about 1610, named the previous king as Gembe (Njimbe), and a Dutch description of 1625 said a ruler dead before that date had reigned 60 years from about 1565, so Njimbe was very likely the founder. The people, Bakongo speaking a northern dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongo-language">Kikongo</a>, included the Vili, the Yombe, Kunyi, Lumbu and Babongo Pygmies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Loango's wealth came from trade. In the seventeenth century Vili merchants travelled far in search of commercial opportunities, among the earliest to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines of Mindouli and the territory of Bukkameale, perhaps the Niari valley; early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> records show Loango exporting considerable copper to the European market. The kingdom was also a major producer and exporter of cloth, to the interior and to the Portuguese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>, where thousands of metres of Loango cloth were imported in the early seventeenth century. Free people paid taxes on their persons, land, slaves and livestock, collected by royal officials who sometimes overcharged.</p><p>From the late seventeenth century the Vili also engaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. Loango exported few slaves at first, but eventually did so in considerable quantities; some were acquired locally, many more from the interior. An early trade led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a>, whose merchants sold slaves to Dutch and English shippers to avoid the taxes of Portuguese Luanda, and Vili communities were reported in São Salvador in 1656. By 1683 they operated in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, and Portuguese efforts to stop them failed; the Vili also took up smithing. Their trade reached the Teke Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> beyond, and by the late eighteenth century slaves from the Bobangi area were a significant share of exports. European shippers built no factories: they anchored offshore and dealt with the mafouks, who managed trade in the royal interest.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/congo-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congo-river.jpg" alt="Congo River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_the_Congo_River_near_Kisangani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MONUSCO/Myriam Asmani, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Loango was made by war and alliance. In the founding tradition recorded by Dapper, the small polities of the coast each had their own leader and made war on each other; the founder from Kakongo triumphed through the skilful use of alliances, defeating those who opposed him, particularly Wansa, Kilongo and Piri, the last two of which required two wars to subdue. Once this was done, more northern regions, including Docke and Sette, submitted voluntarily, and the new king moved north to found settlements and his capital. At its height in the seventeenth century Loango's influence extended from Cape St Catherine in the north to almost the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>.</p><p>The kingdom's later wars were largely its own. In 1663 the king was baptised as Afonso by the Capuchin Bernardo Ungaro, against considerable opposition; when he died a non-Christian took over, who was himself overthrown by one of the Christian party in 1665. This civil war was still going on in the 1670s, and the Christian party was in the end defeated, some of its members fleeing abroad, where one, Miguel da Silva, was elected ruler of Ngoyo and reigned there in 1682. Command of the army lay with the Makaka, the royal council's minister of war. Loango kept European power at a distance without fighting it: no shipper established a permanent factory on its coast. Many of the slaves its merchants sold were acquired in the interior rather than taken by Loango's own forces.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In theory the kings of Loango held absolute and even divine power. Njimbe created a rule of succession in place around 1600: the king gave command of four provinces, Kaye, Boke, Selage and Kabango, to members of his family, and the throne rotated among them. When the king died the ruler of Kaye took over, as happened before 1624; the ruler of Boke took his place and the others advanced. By Dapper's description of about 1640 the king ruled closely over a cluster of villages around Loangogiri, each under a noble he appointed; outlying districts such as Mayumba followed their own rules under nobles sent from court, while Gobby had no royal supervision.</p><p>In 1701 the English merchant Nathaniel Uring found the king dead and power in the hands of the Mucundy, the Queen or Chief Governess, who oversaw women's affairs. By 1766, when Proyart's French missionaries arrived, there was no clear succession: anyone born of a princess could aspire to the throne, and the reigning king had taken power after a seven-year interregnum run by a regent, the Mani Boman. The electors, office-holders appointed by the dead king, chose the next. The council had offices: Magovo and Mapouto for foreign affairs, Makaka for war, Mfouka for commerce and Makimba, grand master of waters and forests. The king spent much time hearing cases. The people worshipped Nzambi a Mpungu and many nkisi served by nganga; Christian missions came repeatedly, but no permanent state church took root as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/carmelites"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carmelites.jpg" alt="Carmelites" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/carmelites">Carmelites</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elias%27_Himmelfahrt-DSC2080.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Loango's reach was commercial. Its cloth clothed the interior and the Portuguese colony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the Vili diaspora carried its traders across west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>: into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, where some converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> at São Salvador, into Ndongo-Matamba, where Queen Verónica I bound herself by treaty in 1683 not to deal with them, and inland to the Teke Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>. From the late seventeenth century that network fed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English shippers off the Loango coast, with people from as far as the Bobangi country among those sold by the late eighteenth century.</p><p>The kingdom's engagement with Christianity began almost with its independence, when Diogo I of Kongo sent missionaries; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmelites">Carmelites</a> in 1583 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1603 and 1628 reported conversions, and the Capuchin baptism of 1663 brought in 6,000 subjects before civil war defeated the Christian party. A black Jewish community on the coast, without links to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> elsewhere, drew the interest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> race scientists and of the German Loango Expedition of 1873–76. The constitution itself was remade by trade: Phyllis Martin argues that external commerce, especially the slave trade, enriched some nobles ahead of others, and that councillors who owed their positions to it came to dominate the king through ever longer interregna. The name Vili, attested from the seventeenth century as Mobili, remains that of the people today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vili-people"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vili-people.jpg" alt="Vili people" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vili-people">Vili people</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeune_gar%C3%A7on_de_type_de_race_Bavili_(Congo_Fran%C3%A7ais).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photographer A. Bergeret et Cie (Nancy, France), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom did not fall to an enemy; its monarchy wore away from within. In theory the king named his successor and placed him as ruler of Kaye, but by the eighteenth century there was so much contention over the position that kings died without naming a Ma-Kaye, and the electors took longer and longer to agree. The king who reigned in 1766 had waited seven years for his throne while the Mani Boman governed. Martin reads this as the work of a council enriched by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and strong enough to force the interregna out. After King Buatu died in 1787 no king was elected at all for more than a hundred years. Royal authority passed in part to the Nganga Mvumbi, the priest of the corpse, who oversaw the body of the dead king awaiting burial; several succeeded one another through the late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries.</p><p>Loango was still seeking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 1773 when French missionaries came, but the state ceased to exist during the European colonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in the 1800s, when its lands were divided among the possessions that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, Gabon and Cabinda. Missionaries on the coast at the end of the nineteenth century called its people Bafiote and their language Fiote; the traditions R. E. Dennett collected around 1890 still named Njimbe as the first king. What survived was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vili-people">Vili people</a> themselves, their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongo-language">Kikongo</a> speech and their clans, and the memory of a kingdom whose kings had been chosen in turn from four provinces.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Loango, a Kongo-speaking state of the Vili founded about 1565 on the Atlantic coast north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, grew rich on cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and, from the late seventeenth century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. Its rotating succession broke down under a council enriched by that trade, and after 1787 no king was elected before European colonisation ended it.</em></p>

